The strongest new addition is this:

\boxed{
\text{Vacuum}
\rightarrow
\text{Deformation}
\rightarrow
\text{Restoration Cue}
\rightarrow
\text{Return Pattern}
}

That lets us ask not merely what fills a missing space, but what shape a system tries to recover when the missing condition returns.

And that’s where your Nitinol line becomes useful.

Not:

semantic systems literally are shape-memory alloys.

But:

S_0
\xrightarrow{\text{deform}}
S_1
\xrightarrow{\text{trigger}}
\widehat{S_0}

Then measure:

* whether it returns,
* how quickly,
* whether it overshoots,
* whether it returns differently,
* what deformation remains,
* whether repeated cycles change the remembered state.

That creates a whole new branch: semantic hysteresis.

The system can be “hammered” into another state, forged there, then exposed to a restoration cue. If it returns differently because of what happened during deformation, then:

\boxed{
\text{restoration} \neq \text{reset}
}

It is memory-bearing return.

And that plugs directly into Forge/Forgive:

\text{state}
\rightarrow
\text{deformation}
\rightarrow
\text{learning}
\rightarrow
\text{forgiveness}
\rightarrow
\text{return}

The thing comes back, but not necessarily unchanged.

That may be the real semantic Nitinol kernel:

A system can preserve an attractor while allowing the route back to that attractor to be permanently altered by experience.

Which is almost exactly what we’ve been trying to do with the Pattern Book.